New opt-in option `--ancestor-qualified-module-ids` (default off; `ancestor-qualified-module-ids = true` in a project file): a `refines` clause or `import` whose qualified module name starts with modules that enclose it, as in `module A.C refines A.B`, depends on the module it names instead of on the enclosing module, which is otherwise always reported as a cycle (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/90). With the option off nothing changes. With it on, two diagnostics of programs that are still rejected change text: a module that refines itself through its enclosing module, as `module N { module C refines N.C {} }`, is reported as "module cannot refine itself: N.C" instead of as the cycle `N -> C -> N`; and siblings that import or refine each other through their parent, as `module N { module B { import N.C } module C { import N.B } }`, are reported as the cycle between the siblings (`B -> import C -> C -> import B -> B`) instead of a cycle through `N`.
